00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:15.500
In 2015, a bombshell report came out detailing
how the TSA, the agency responsible for airport

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:22.180
security in the US, missed 95% of drugs, explosives,
weapons, and other prohibited items sent through

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.010
their scanners in a test.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:28.369
In 2016, the test was repeated with the same
result.

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:34.130
The following year, 2017, the test was repeated
again with an improved success rate, but still,

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.320
it let 70% of prohibited items through.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.520
In fact, within the US, there’s no evidence
that the TSA has ever prevented a terrorist

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.010
attack and outside the US, there are very
few examples of physical airport security

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.660
thwarting an attack.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.390
Airport security slows people down, increases
cost, and yet does little to actually improve

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:58.490
safety so it’s safe to say that airport
security is broken, but why?

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:02.600
Since the purpose of aviation is to get from
one place to another, it’s tough for a single

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.610
country to regulate the industry.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.710
Instead, the International Civil Aviation
Organization, a United Nations agency, does.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:15.040
All but two UN member countries, Liechtenstein
and Dominica, are part of the ICAO so its

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.610
regulations are more or less universal.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.210
They’re the ones responsible for making
sure that air transport works the same way

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:27.229
all around the world, but what they require
for airport security is rather inconcrete.

00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:31.810
They simply say, “measures need to be established
to prevent weapons, explosives or any other

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.640
dangerous devices, articles or substances,
which may be used to commit an act of unlawful

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.210
interference, the carriage and bearing of
which is not authorized, from being introduced,

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.140
by any means whatsoever, on board an aircraft
engaged in civil aviation.”

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.840
Essentially, all they require is that before
a passenger gets onboard a plane, the airport

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:50.560
assures they don’t have weapons.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.000
While the ICAO does determine whether this
requirement has been fulfilled, it’s up

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.130
to each country to decide how they achieve
that.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.310
In the US, airport security works like this.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.890
After check-in, a passenger first goes to
have their documents checked, puts their bags

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:09.140
through an x-ray scanner, and walks through
either a metal detector or millimeter wave

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.140
scanner.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.290
There are potentially additional steps such
as a pat-down or explosives residue test for

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.680
some, but these three steps are how the process
works for most.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.341
It is though, important to note that, if you
so choose, you can skip the metal detector

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:27.180
or millimeter wave scanner step of the process
and receive a pat-down instead.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.480
This is fully compliant with ICAO regulations
since it does effectively screen an individual

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.790
for weapons.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:39.190
In fact, an airport could hypothetically be
compliant with ICAO regulations only by conducting

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.540
physical bag-searches and pat-downs since
this does screen for weapons even if this

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.360
would be slow, invasive, and unpopular.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.630
Some small airports actually do this for bags—they
physically search them instead of passing

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:52.300
them through an x-ray.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.660
The words “security theater” are thrown
around a lot in regards to US airport security.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.580
This term is used pejoratively—it means
that the TSA’s function is only to make

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:07.220
airports look secure, but here’s the thing—on
paper, the TSA has done its job.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:12.230
There has not been a single death on a flight
leaving from a US airport since 9/11 as a

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.820
result of terrorism.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:18.370
In fact, in the same time period, there has
not even been a single attempted terrorist

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:23.890
attack on a flight leaving from a US airport—all
have been on flights originating from abroad.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.930
So maybe the TSA is doing its function.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:31.020
Maybe the mere threat of being caught has
been enough to thwart terrorists or maybe

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.831
terrorists have moved on from attacking airplanes.

00:03:32.831 --> 00:03:39.790
The US is certainly a prime target for terrorism,
but there are more controversial nations.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.940
Israeli airport security is considered to
be the best in the world.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:48.480
Despite being situated in one of the most
politically contentious countries in the world,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:54.030
no airplane leaving from Tel Aviv’s Ben
Gurion airport has ever been hijacked or bombed.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:58.939
It’s been attempted, but this airport’s
security is simply nearly impenetrable.

00:03:58.939 --> 00:04:05.040
What’s most fascinating is that this airport
is hardly using any super-advanced technology—they

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.430
use the exact same metal detectors as the
US and Europe—but they’ve focused on the

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:10.879
human factor.

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:15.760
Israel has come to realize that its unfortunately
easy to get weapons through airport security.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.500
Plastic explosives, non-metallic knives, and
blunt weapons are just tough to detect so

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:25.490
rather than focusing on the weapons that could
be used for an attack, they focus on the people

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:26.490
who could use them.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:31.830
At Ben Gurion airport, security starts before
passengers even get to the airport.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.620
Cars pass through a security checkpoint a
mile away from the airport entrance where

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.520
guards inspect cars and look for any suspicious
looking individuals.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.520
The minute passengers arrive at the airport,
they’re being watched already.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.720
Highly trained plainclothes officers roam
around in the check-in area again searching

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.680
for individuals acting abnormally or nervous.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.390
Then, before passengers are even allowed to
check-in, there’s the interview.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:05:00.110
Anyone who’s passed through Ben Gurion airport
knows how intense this interview is but its

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:05.220
likely the single greatest factor leading
to the safety of Israeli airports.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.250
Passengers are first asked standard questions
like what their jobs are, why they came to

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:13.830
Israel, how long they stayed for, if they
packed their own bags and all the while, the

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.460
security agent watches the passengers face
for reactions.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:22.530
They’ll also probe deeper—asking why the
passenger has been to various countries stamped

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.530
in their passport.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:28.760
They’ll then ask oddly specific questions—which
school they went to, when they last moved,

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.370
what kind of car they have.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:33.820
These are all to see if the passenger is being
honest about who they are.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:38.460
If the security officer senses hesitation
or finds a hole in their story, they could

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.800
deem the passenger a higher risk.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.270
Once passengers have completed their interview,
a barcode is placed on the back of their passport

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.020
that starts with a number from one to six.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:52.289
If it starts with a one, the passenger is
deemed a very low risk—this is almost only

00:05:52.289 --> 00:05:57.110
given to Israeli citizens—while if it starts
with six, the passenger is deemed a very high

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:00.060
risk and will be subjected to extremely thorough
screening.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:05.330
While a lot of the risk determination has
to do with the interview, profiling also plays

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:06.330
into it.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:10.110
According to the Israeli security system,
passengers are deemed higher risk if they

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:15.130
are male, if they are traveling alone, if
they are young, and most of all, if they are

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:16.130
Arab.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:21.130
Israel unapologetically uses racial profiling
in their risk assessment which has led to

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:22.630
international condemnation.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:26.580
Israel, meanwhile, argues that this technique
is effective.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:31.140
After all, the country has recently been at
war with many of its Arabian neighbors.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.449
At the same time, though, its almost impossible
for an Arab traveller to pass through Ben

00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:39.639
Gurion airport without getting deemed a number
six security risk.

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:44.639
Western non-Israeli individuals are also generally
considered to be a higher security risk typically

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:47.110
receiving a four or a five.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.979
After the interview, passengers are finally
allowed to check-in.

00:06:49.979 --> 00:06:55.130
Their checked bags are put through a standard
x-ray and then are placed in a pressure chamber.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:58.630
The chamber’s pressure is lowered to the
level of a pressurized aircraft, about the

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:03.110
equivalent of six to eight thousand feet of
altitude to set off any explosives designed

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:06.900
to trigger when a plane’s cabin pressure
is lower at cruising altitude.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.830
Meanwhile, the passenger passes through a
standard x-ray or body scanner.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.210
For the majority of passengers, this physical
screening process is exactly the same as it

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:19.099
would be in North America, Europe, or Asia—they
just walk through the scanners and grab their

00:07:19.099 --> 00:07:20.099
bags.

00:07:20.099 --> 00:07:24.419
Those deemed a higher security risk—above
three or four—likely would have their bags

00:07:24.419 --> 00:07:29.539
manually searched and then the highest risk
individuals—five or sixes—are often taken

00:07:29.539 --> 00:07:32.770
aside for another round of questioning and
a pat-down.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:36.630
The security doesn’t even stop once passengers
board the plane.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:42.110
Like the US, Israel has a system of air marshals—armed
security guards on planes—but unlike the

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.979
US, at least in the case of El Al, Israel’s
national airline, there are air marshals on

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:48.180
every single flight.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:52.240
They sit among the passengers, often near
any that were identified at the airport as

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.300
high risk, and they have alert buttons that
communicate with the pilots in case of an

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:57.729
attempted hijacking.

00:07:57.729 --> 00:08:01.800
If the marshals press this button, an alarm
will go off in the cockpit and the pilots

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.139
will often send the plane into a dive to knock
the hijacker off their feet.

00:08:06.139 --> 00:08:10.510
This technique has successfully prevented
terror attacks in the past.

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:15.599
These air marshals secure the plane from the
inside, but another system protects the outside.

00:08:15.599 --> 00:08:19.790
El Al’s planes are installed with thermal
flares that deploy when a radar detects an

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:21.479
incoming missile.

00:08:21.479 --> 00:08:24.919
Thermal guided missiles will then target the
flares instead of the plane.

00:08:24.919 --> 00:08:29.259
El Al and the other Israeli airlines also
stay secure by having security officers at

00:08:29.259 --> 00:08:31.150
their destination airports.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:36.599
For departing passengers to Israel, they repeat
much of same security process as at Ben Gurion

00:08:36.599 --> 00:08:41.500
Airport—conducting interviews, profiling,
and assessing the risk of each passenger.

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:45.769
At many foreign airports, these agents also
physically screen luggage before handing it

00:08:45.769 --> 00:08:48.120
off to the regular airport security.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.240
El Al, despite being the flag carrier of one
of the most controversial nations in the world,

00:08:53.240 --> 00:09:00.540
had its last and only hijacking in 1968 and
even this incident resulted in zero deaths.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:06.000
It and the Ben Gurion airport are testaments
to the fact that truly secure security is

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.850
theoretically possible, but is it possible
worldwide?

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:14.060
Here’s the thing about Tel Aviv’s airport—its
not that big.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:19.120
20 million passengers pass through it each
year making it only as busy as San Diego or

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.670
Berlin airport.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:25.040
These are not small airports, but they’re
not on the same scale as the world’s largest

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.339
like JFK, Heathrow, or Dubai.

00:09:27.339 --> 00:09:31.920
It’s tough to determine if a system like
what Israel has implemented could scale up

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.270
to be used universally.

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:37.960
What’s sure is that certain elements of
the Israeli system would not work—most countries

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:42.040
could not justify a system that relies so
heavily on racial profiling.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:47.649
In many countries and US states, practices
such as this are simply illegal, but still,

00:09:47.649 --> 00:09:52.150
airports and countries around the world are
watching Israeli security methods closely.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:56.440
The US has already implemented a system of
security interviews for many international

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:01.860
flights to the US however these are generally
conducted by less trained contract workers.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:07.149
Brussels airport, after its terrorist bombing
in 2016, now positions officers trained in

00:10:07.149 --> 00:10:09.660
behavioral detection at its entrances.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:14.140
Plenty of other airports have sent delegates
to Ben Gurion airport to evaluate their security

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:18.570
techniques as well and have quietly made changes
to closer emulate Israel.

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:23.570
But few ever stop to question if we even want
an increase in airport security.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:27.140
If it comes at the expense of time, maybe
we don’t.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:32.740
As mentioned, the US has not had a successful
terror attack on an airplane since 9/11.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:39.810
Worldwide, airplane hijackings are now almost
nonexistent, but security has a consequence.

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:44.550
In the most direct way, tickets for every
single flight leaving from a US airport include

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:48.399
a $5.60 fee that goes towards paying for security.

00:10:48.399 --> 00:10:53.680
This may n ot be much in the scope of a multi-hundred
dollar flight to Europe or Asia, but if the

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.730
US ever wants to get to the point where Europe
is of having $10 or $20 budget airline tickets

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:02.350
between domestic destinations, this fee has
to go.

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:07.100
A study found that the TSA’s average cost
per life saved—how much money it spends

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:12.820
to stop one human death—is $667 million.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:16.670
You can certainly say that you can’t put
a price on a human life, but the security

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:20.140
that saves these lives costs lives.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:25.610
The plane is empirically the safest way to
travel—its hundreds or thousands of times

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:31.050
safer than driving—so stopping people from
flying is in and of itself deadly.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:36.950
Economists found that the increase in airport
security in the US post 9/11 can account for

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.089
6% of the decline in air travel.

00:11:39.089 --> 00:11:45.260
Given that, in 2002, more than 500 people
died because, as a result of longer security

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:51.120
times and more extensive searches, they chose
to drive over fly and were involved in a fatal

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:52.120
accident.

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:57.500
Flying being easy helps everyone—it lets
people travel faster and it helps airlines

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:02.170
as businesses, just as long as it doesn’t
result in a decrease in safety.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:07.640
In Israel, passengers arrive three hours before
their flights just to clear security which

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:12.029
means plane travel is inefficient while in
some places in Europe, airports encourage

00:12:12.029 --> 00:12:15.360
travelers to arrive a mere hour before their
flight.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:19.740
The goal is to keep security fast, efficient,
but also secure.

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:25.050
No country or airport has the perfect system
but to achieve this, one likely needs to combine

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:29.519
elements of the strictest security systems
with those of the fastest.

00:12:29.519 --> 00:12:34.450
Israel can get away with invasive, unethical,
yet effective security measures because they

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:35.470
need to.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:38.750
They are a country seemingly constantly at
war.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:42.700
Many other areas of the world just aren’t
big targets for terrorists, though.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:46.209
Its hard to know whether or not security measures
are effective.

00:12:46.209 --> 00:12:50.650
There’s no control group of developed countries
that don’t have airport security to compare

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:51.650
to.

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:56.170
The most effective security systems stop attacks
by the mere threat of consequences rather

00:12:56.170 --> 00:13:00.670
than through physical screening so maybe airport
security works.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:06.589
In the US and Europe and Asia and all the
other developed, safe areas of the world,

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:12.959
one must therefore ask whether airport security
is actually saving lives or ending them.

